{"name":"English Channel","hex":"#4e6173","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"English Channel","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["The arm of the Atlantic Ocean that separates the British Isles from northern France, and joins the North Sea to the Atlantic Ocean."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/English_Channel"}],"entries":[{"title":"English Channel","blocks":[{"text":"The English Channel, also known as the Channel, is an arm of the Atlantic Ocean that separates Southern England from northern France. It links to the southern part of the North Sea by the Strait of Dover at its northeastern end. It is the busiest shipping area in the world."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7a/English_Channel_Satellite.jpg/330px-English_Channel_Satellite.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/English_Channel"},{"title":"English Channel (horse)","blocks":[{"text":"English Channel was an American Champion Thoroughbred racehorse and a leading sire of turf horses in North America. In four years of racing, he competed in 23 races, winning 13, finishing second in four, and finishing third in one. His final victory came at the 2007 Breeders' Cup Turf, where he set a record for this race when he won by seven lengths. Following this race, he was retired to stud in Lexington, Kentucky."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/68/EnglishChannelBreedersCup2007.jpg/330px-EnglishChannelBreedersCup2007.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/English_Channel_(horse)"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/19/Osborne-iow-3Ja10-10876.jpg/500px-Osborne-iow-3Ja10-10876.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/19/Osborne-iow-3Ja10-10876.jpg/500px-Osborne-iow-3Ja10-10876.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Osborne House, the summer retreat of Queen Victoria on the Isle of Wight. Starting from the late 18th century, settlements on and around the English Channel coastline in England grew rapidly into thriving seaside resorts, bolstered by their association with royalty and the middle and upper classes.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AOsborne-iow-3Ja10-10876.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7b/Carte_de_la_Manche.png/500px-Carte_de_la_Manche.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7b/Carte_de_la_Manche.png/500px-Carte_de_la_Manche.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Map of the channel area with French nomenclature","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACarte_de_la_Manche.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b8/English_Channel.jpg/330px-English_Channel.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b8/English_Channel.jpg/330px-English_Channel.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Map of the English Channel","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AEnglish_Channel.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fe/FranceGrisNez2Dover.jpg/500px-FranceGrisNez2Dover.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fe/FranceGrisNez2Dover.jpg/1280px-FranceGrisNez2Dover.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fe/FranceGrisNez2Dover.jpg/500px-FranceGrisNez2Dover.jpg?utm_source=en.w